--- body ---
## Context

SPEC.md §5.2.8 decides every gate at one of two levels: the fixture level,
on the committable corpus, and the library level, against the author's
real library, which cannot be committed. The Menagerie (ticket 0029) is the
fixture level. The library level has so far been a manual act. The author
named the missing instrument while ruling on the panel of 2026-09-06: a
system that generates about a hundred questions on the fly from the real
library and benches the search engine against them.

Why it is worth a deliverable of its own. A pinned bank can be tuned
against by whoever tunes the engine; a bank regenerated at each run from a
private library cannot. The Menagerie is representative in shape by
construction against the census (ticket 0711); the real library is
representative by being the thing itself. And the same scoring applies to
both, ruling 7 of 2026-09-06: an answer is a paragraph in a page in a file
attached to a Zotero entry, and a reply scores win, near-win or miss on the
rank of that paragraph and the completeness of its citation chain (title,
author, date, identifier, section heading, printed page number, and the
part's title and byline for a compound document).

## What it is

1. Sample about a hundred answer paragraphs from the real library's
   extracted text, stratified so the sample follows the library's own
   marginals (item type, format, language, length, notes and annotations),
   with the full citation chain read from Zotero's metadata.
2. Author one question per paragraph with a local model, in the paragraph's
   language and, for a declared share, in another of R7's languages, so the
   cross-lingual pairs of ruling 2 appear on the fly.
3. Run the engine under test through the acceptance harness's query verb,
   score each reply on rank and chain completeness, and report per lane
   (question language against answer-paragraph language), per format and
   per stratum, with counts beside rates.
4. Regenerate at each run. A run records its sample seed, the library
   version, and the run identity block of the fixture contract, so a
   comparison across runs is honest about what changed.

## Constraints

- R10 and R36 hold: generation runs on a local model, no text leaves the
  machine, no paid service.
- Read-only against Zotero and the library, as the census is.
- A question written from a paragraph it must retrieve is a self-consistency
  probe, closer to a recall check than to a user need; say so in the
  report, and reserve need-driven questions for the Menagerie.
- Nothing from a run is committed except aggregates: no paragraph text, no
  titles, no keys.

## Relevant files

- `bench/library_census.py` (ticket 0711) — the stratification marginals
  and the read-only access pattern
- `bench/acceptance/interface.py` — the query verb every adapter offers
- `bench/golden_gate.py` — the scorer's readings; the rank and chain
  scoring is new and belongs beside it, not inside it
- `conception/0029-golden-fixture-structure-review.md` Parts C to E — the
  rulings that define the answer and the score

## Exit criteria
- [ ] a generator that produces about a hundred questions from the real
      library in one run, stratified against the census marginals, with the
      full citation chain per answer
- [ ] a scorer reading win, near-win and miss on rank and chain completeness
- [ ] a run against the reviewed zoteus build reported in `verification/`,
      aggregates only, with its seed and library version
- [ ] README's deliverables table gains the row, once the author confirms
      the name
